  "coverage": [
    "A separate bridge peer dies while its watcher is stopped; orphan-group hangup triggers cleanup rather than losing the loaned locks.",
    "An original child closes inherited descriptors and ignores SIGTERM. Directory reuse occurs only after that child is independently observed absent or zombie, with no later write after a controlled gate opens.",
    "Confirmed claim release preserves the warm server; explicit stop confirms managed-group termination.",
    "Cancelling a folder handoff before acknowledgement closes the original control channel while the warm owner remains retained, so the independent watcher cannot keep an abandoned loan indefinitely.",
    "All 64 passed descriptors arrive close-on-exec; malformed reply decoding drops received descriptors. The complete public argument budget survives JSON framing.",
    "Existing lifecycle, warm handoff, context checks, cancellation, source compilation, both authenticated transports and all eight pinned real-server contracts pass."
  ],
  "development_findings": [
    {
      "observation": "The added cancellation regression failed on the previous implementation: cancelling the unacknowledged retain future left the retained warm owner control channel open. Constructing the cleanup guard before awaiting the handoff now shuts that channel on every incomplete return or cancellation.",
      "log_sha256": "f2977415b6e4d5ba7d4889d8127f950c022348df081f2c5666220767309caef4"
    },
    {
      "observation": "The initial stopped-watcher crash test exposed default SIGHUP terminating the watcher after its parent died, leaving its original server/child alive. Signal-safe stop handlers now perform group cleanup. The final test uses a deterministic write gate and independent process inspection.",
      "log_sha256": "72d4e4d1153ee320ae9a9854bc0b03dc98717cc114f2ccf269268c800d4396b2"
    },
